{"graph_chars": "Bring it home: nurses. Median salary in two thousand twenty, seventy-five thousand three hundred thirty dollars. Baby-boomer healthcare needs shift nurse demand right: higher wage, more nurses hired. If supply also shrinks because nurses retire and tuition rises, supply shifts left: wages higher still, and the net change in quantity is ambiguous — it depends which shift is larger. We know salaries rise. Recitation. In the labor market, households supply and firms demand. Demand for labor is derived from the product. Own wage is a movement. Shifters include output demand, training, technology, number of firms, rules, and other inputs. Supply shifters: number of workers, required education, policy. Technology can cut low-skill demand and raise high-skill demand. A living wage is a floor. Binding floors above equilibrium create surplus labor. A minimum set below equilibrium is nonbinding. In financial markets the price is the interest rate. Savers supply, borrowers demand. Fifteen percent and six hundred billion in the card example. Usury is a ceiling. Don't kill the messenger. That is Chapter Four.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0654], [0.0654, 0.1309], [0.1309, 0.1963], [0.1963, 0.2618], [0.2618, 0.3272], [0.3272, 0.3927], [0.3927, 0.4581], [0.4581, 0.5236], [0.5236, 0.589], [0.589, 0.6544], [0.6544, 0.7199], [0.7199, 0.7853], [0.7853, 0.8508], [0.8508, 0.9162], [0.9162, 0.9817], [0.9817, 1.0471], [1.0471, 1.1126], [1.1126, 1.178], [1.178, 1.2435], [1.2435, 1.3089], [1.3089, 1.3743], [1.3743, 1.4398], [1.4398, 1.5052], [1.5052, 1.5707], [1.5707, 1.6361], [1.6361, 1.7016], [1.7016, 1.767], [1.767, 1.8325], [1.8325, 1.8979], [1.8979, 1.9633], [1.9633, 2.0288], [2.0288, 2.0942], [2.0942, 2.1597], [2.1597, 2.2251], [2.2251, 2.2906], [2.2906, 2.356], [2.356, 2.4215], [2.4215, 2.4869], [2.4869, 2.5523], [2.5523, 2.6178], [2.6178, 2.6832], [2.6832, 2.7487], [2.7487, 2.8141], [2.8141, 2.8796], [2.8796, 2.945], [2.945, 3.0105], [3.0105, 3.0759], [3.0759, 3.1413], 